# Transient Heat Transfer Analysis Using an Implicit Numerical Method

## 1. Objective

The objective of this analysis is to determine whether increasing the effective thermal conductivity of the soil reduces the cooling time of a steel plate resting on the ground.

The original Exercise 5-30E provides a steady-state heat transfer problem. To evaluate cooling time, the problem was extended to a transient heat transfer model by incorporating thermal energy accumulation.

Two cases were analyzed:

- **Original case:** Soil thermal conductivity of 0.49 Btu/(h·ft·°F).
- **Modified case:** Soil thermal conductivity of 1.50 Btu/(h·ft·°F).

Both cases were simulated using identical initial temperatures, material heat capacities, geometry, and boundary conditions. The cooling time was defined as the time required for the steel-soil interface (node 5) to decrease from 80°F to 75°F.

## 2. Problem Data and Assumptions

The following properties were retained from the original exercise:

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Steel plate thickness | 5 in |
| Soil depth | 3 ft |
| Steel thermal conductivity | 7.2 Btu/(h·ft·°F) |
| Original soil conductivity | 0.49 Btu/(h·ft·°F) |
| Modified soil conductivity | 1.50 Btu/(h·ft·°F) |
| Ambient air temperature | 80°F |
| Effective sky temperature | 510°R |
| Convection coefficient | 3.5 Btu/(h·ft²·°F) |
| Surface emissivity | 0.6 |
| Bottom boundary temperature | 50°F |

Additional representative properties were assumed because the original steady-state exercise did not provide density or specific heat:

| Material | Density (kg/m³) | Specific heat (J/kg·K) |
|---|---:|---:|
| Steel | 7,850 | 490 |
| Soil | 1,300 | 830 |

The transient model also assumes:

- One-dimensional heat transfer.
- Constant thermal properties.
- Perfect thermal contact between the steel and soil.
- No internal heat generation.
- An initially uniform temperature of 80°F at nodes 0–9.
- A constant temperature of 50°F at node 10.
- A target temperature of 75°F at node 5.

The initial and target temperatures are additional assumptions introduced specifically for the transient comparison.

## 3. Governing Energy Balance

The first law of thermodynamics is expressed as:

\[
\dot E_{in}-\dot E_{out}+\dot E_{gen}=\dot E_{acc}
\]

Since there is no internal heat generation:

\[
\dot E_{in}-\dot E_{out}=\dot E_{acc}
\]

The thermal energy accumulation term is:

\[
\dot E_{acc}=\rho VC_p\frac{dT}{dt}
\]

Dividing by the cross-sectional area:

\[
q''_{in}-q''_{out}
=\rho C_p\frac{V}{A}\frac{dT}{dt}
\]

This relationship was applied to the control volume surrounding each node.

## 4. Thermal Properties and Nodal Heat Capacities

All calculations were performed using SI units.

The spatial increments are:

\[
\Delta x_s=1\ \text{in}=0.0254\ \text{m}
\]

\[
\Delta x_g=0.6\ \text{ft}=0.18288\ \text{m}
\]

