\]

## 5. Transient Finite-Difference Equations

### 5.1. Node 0: Upper Steel Surface

The upper surface exchanges heat through convection, radiation, and conduction.

\[
C_0''\frac{dT_0}{dt}
=h(T_\infty-T_0)
+\varepsilon\sigma(T_{sky}^4-T_0^4)
+G_s''(T_1-T_0)
\]

Substituting the known coefficients:

\[
\begin{aligned}
48,850.55\frac{dT_0}{dt}
={}&19.8739(299.8167-T_0)\\
&+0.6(5.670374419\times10^{-8})(283.3333^4-T_0^4)\\
&+490.602(T_1-T_0)
\end{aligned}
\]

Absolute temperatures in the radiation term are expressed in kelvin.

### 5.2. Nodes 1–4: Steel Interior

\[
C_s''\frac{dT_i}{dt}
=G_s''(T_{i-1}-2T_i+T_{i+1})
\]

Therefore:

\[
97,701.10\frac{dT_i}{dt}
=490.602(T_{i-1}-2T_i+T_{i+1})
\]

for \(i=1,2,3,4\).

### 5.3. Node 5: Steel-Soil Interface

The interface receives heat from the steel and exchanges heat with the soil.

\[
C_5''\frac{dT_5}{dt}
=G_s''(T_4-T_5)+G_g''(T_6-T_5)
\]

**Original case:**

\[
\boxed{
147,514.31\frac{dT_5}{dt}
=490.602(T_4-T_5)+4.63725(T_6-T_5)
}
\]

**Modified case:**

\[
\boxed{
147,514.31\frac{dT_5}{dt}
=490.602(T_4-T_5)+14.19566(T_6-T_5)
}
\]

The change in soil thermal conductivity directly affects the conductive term between nodes 5 and 6.

### 5.4. Nodes 6–9: Soil Interior

\[
C_g''\frac{dT_i}{dt}
=G_g''(T_{i-1}-2T_i+T_{i+1})
\]

**Original case:**

\[
197,327.52\frac{dT_i}{dt}
=4.63725(T_{i-1}-2T_i+T_{i+1})
\]

**Modified case:**

\[
197,327.52\frac{dT_i}{dt}
=14.19566(T_{i-1}-2T_i+T_{i+1})
\]

for \(i=6,7,8,9\).

The bottom boundary is maintained at:

\[
T_{10}(t)=50^\circ F
\]

## 6. Implicit Numerical Integration

The transient equations form a system of ten coupled ordinary differential equations.

An implicit integration method was used to calculate the temperature evolution.

### 6.1. Backward Euler Formulation

The time derivative is approximated using:

\[
\frac{dT_i}{dt}
\approx\frac{T_i^{n+1}-T_i^n}{\Delta t}
\]

Unlike an explicit method, Backward Euler evaluates the heat transfer terms at the unknown future time \(n+1\).

For node 5:

\[
\begin{aligned}
C_5''\frac{T_5^{n+1}-T_5^n}{\Delta t}
={}&G_s''(T_4^{n+1}-T_5^{n+1})\\
&+G_g''(T_6^{n+1}-T_5^{n+1})
\end{aligned}
\]

Rearranging:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\left(1+\frac{\Delta t(G_s''+G_g'')}{C_5''}\right)T_5^{n+1}\\
&-\frac{\Delta tG_s''}{C_5''}T_4^{n+1}
-\frac{\Delta tG_g''}{C_5''}T_6^{n+1}
=T_5^n
\end{aligned}
\]

### 6.2. First Time Step: Original Case

For a time step of one hour:

\[
\Delta t=3600\ \text{s}
\]

The steel coefficient is:

\[
a_s=\frac{3600(490.602)}{147,514.31}
=11.97285
\]

The original soil coefficient is:

\[
a_{g,o}=\frac{3600(4.63725)}{147,514.31}
=0.11317
\]

Substitution gives:

\[
\boxed{
13.08602T_5^1
-11.97285T_4^1
-0.11317T_6^1=80
}
\]

### 6.3. First Time Step: Modified Case

The modified soil coefficient is:

\[
a_{g,m}=\frac{3600(14.19566)}{147,514.31}
=0.34644
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
13.31929T_5^1
-11.97285T_4^1
-0.34644T_6^1=80
}
\]

These equations cannot be solved independently because the temperatures of neighboring nodes are also unknown.

Consequently, all ten transient equations must be solved simultaneously.

## 7. Nonlinear System and Newton-Raphson Method

The unknown nodal temperatures are arranged into the vector:

\[
\mathbf T^{n+1}
=
[T_0^{n+1},T_1^{n+1},\ldots,T_9^{n+1}]^T
\]

The implicit equations can be expressed as:

\[
\mathbf F(\mathbf T^{n+1})
=\mathbf T^{n+1}-\mathbf T^n
-\Delta t\,\mathbf f(\mathbf T^{n+1})=\mathbf0
\]

Because the upper surface contains the nonlinear radiation term \(T_0^4\), the complete system is nonlinear.

Newton-Raphson can be applied:

\[
\mathbf J(\mathbf T^{(j)})\Delta\mathbf T
=-\mathbf F(\mathbf T^{(j)})
\]

\[
\mathbf T^{(j+1)}
=\mathbf T^{(j)}+\Delta\mathbf T
\]

where \(\mathbf J\) is the Jacobian matrix.

For the upper surface, the diagonal Jacobian element is:

\[
J_{00}
=1+\frac{\Delta t}{C_0''}
\left(h+4\varepsilon\sigma T_0^3+G_s''\right)
\]

The neighboring-node element is:

\[
J_{01}=-\frac{\Delta tG_s''}{C_0''}
\]

The numerical solver repeats the iterations until the residuals of the energy-balance equations are sufficiently small.

## 8. Cooling-Time Determination

The initial temperatures are:

\[
T_i(0)=80^\circ F,\quad i=0,\ldots,9
\]

The target temperature at the steel-soil interface is:

\[
T_5(t)=75^\circ F
\]

The numerical solution is evaluated until the target is reached.

For a fixed-step method, if the temperature crosses 75°F between two consecutive time steps, linear interpolation can be used:

\[
\boxed{
t_{75}=t_n+
\frac{T_5^n-75}{T_5^n-T_5^{n+1}}\Delta t
}
\]

For example, with Backward Euler and a one-hour step in the original case:

\[
T_5(70\ \text{h})\approx75.003081^\circ F
\]

\[
T_5(71\ \text{h})\approx74.990010^\circ F
\]

Therefore:

\[
t_{75}
=70+\frac{75.003081-75}{75.003081-74.990010}(1)
\]

\[
\boxed{t_{75}\approx70.236\ \text{h}}
\]

This is the approximate crossing time obtained using a one-hour Backward Euler time step.

## 9. Time-Step Convergence

The numerical calculation was repeated using progressively smaller time steps.

| Numerical method | Original cooling time (h) | Modified cooling time (h) |
|---|---:|---:|
| Backward Euler, Δt = 1 h | 70.236 | 20.293 |
| Backward Euler, Δt = 0.1 h | 69.368 | 19.834 |
| Backward Euler, Δt = 0.01 h | 69.281 | 19.788 |
| Adaptive BDF reference solution | 69.271 | 19.783 |

The results approach consistent values as the time step decreases.

The adaptive BDF method uses variable time steps and, when appropriate, higher-order backward differentiation formulas. It is related to Backward Euler but is not identical to fixed-step first-order integration.

## 10. Cooling-Time Comparison

The reference numerical results are:

**Original case:**

\[
\boxed{t_o=69.271\ \text{h}}
\]

**Modified case:**

\[
\boxed{t_m=19.783\ \text{h}}
\]

The reduction in cooling time is:

\[
\%\text{ reduction}
=\frac{t_o-t_m}{t_o}(100)
\]

Substituting:

\[
=\frac{69.271-19.783}{69.271}(100)
\]

\[
=\frac{49.488}{69.271}(100)
\]

\[
\boxed{\%\text{ reduction}=71.44\%}
\]

The time saved is:

\[
\Delta t=69.271-19.783
\]

\[
\boxed{\Delta t=49.488\ \text{h}}
\]

The ratio of cooling times is:

\[
\frac{69.271}{19.783}\approx3.50
\]

Therefore, under the selected transient conditions, the modified system reaches the target temperature approximately 3.50 times faster.

