These steady-state values provide a reference for checking the long-time behavior of the transient model.

Small numerical differences may occur because of rounding and the precision used for physical constants.

## 12. Discussion

Increasing the soil thermal conductivity from 0.49 to 1.50 Btu/(h·ft·°F) increases the ability of the soil to conduct thermal energy toward the fixed-temperature lower boundary.

In the original system, the soil presents a relatively high thermal resistance, limiting heat transfer through the ground.

In the modified system, the lower conductive resistance allows thermal energy to be transported more effectively.

Although the modified system does not necessarily have a lower temperature at every instant during the transient, it reaches the selected interface temperature of 75°F significantly earlier.

The numerical results indicate that the cooling time decreases from approximately 69.27 hours to 19.78 hours, representing a 71.44% reduction.

This improvement is specific to the assumed material properties, initial temperature distribution, boundary conditions, and target temperature. A different thermal scenario could produce a different percentage improvement.

## 13. Conclusion

A transient finite-difference model was developed from the first law of thermodynamics to investigate the effect of soil thermal conductivity on the cooling response of a steel plate resting on the ground.

The model included conduction through the steel and soil, convection and radiation at the upper surface, and thermal energy accumulation in each control volume.

Two numerical simulations were conducted using identical initial and boundary conditions, with soil conductivity as the only modified parameter.

The original system required approximately **69.27 hours** for the steel-soil interface to cool from 80°F to 75°F, whereas the modified system required approximately **19.78 hours**.

The resulting **71.44% reduction in cooling time** demonstrates that increasing the effective thermal conductivity of the soil can substantially improve the cooling performance under the specified conditions.

The transient analysis extends the original steady-state exercise by introducing representative thermal storage properties and explicit initial and target temperatures. The numerical integration results should therefore be interpreted within these modeling assumptions.
